\section{Represent a Closure as Code Plus an Environment}

A host closure can refer to its lexical environment. A C
function pointer names an entry that native code can call under a
specified convention; it does not carry the lexical environment
the VM uses internally. The Common Lisp extract describes
declaring an entry callable from C, including its return type.
Such a declaration solves the direction and representation of the
machine call, not the question of which closure should receive it.
Prefer a C API that accepts a function pointer and a context
pointer together. Then many registrations can share one compiled
entry point and each context identifies its own host closure.

For example, a native file catalog receives a visitor as
\texttt{int visit(const char *, size\_t, void *)}.
The native code lends UTF-8 filename bytes for the duration
of each visit and interprets the integer as continue or stop.
The closure has a pattern and a limit in its environment.
The context may contain a stable token to a strong root rather
than an address into the managed heap. Crestani's discussion of
reference objects distinguishes short-lived local references
from explicitly released global references. A registration
that escapes its creating call needs the latter kind of
lifetime, even if the first invocation happens synchronously.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.61\linewidth}}
\hline
Thing & What keeps it valid \\
\hline
Machine entry & Compiled code remains loaded while registered. \\
Context address & Native-owned or stably allocated until quiescence. \\
Closure and captures & Root remains live through the last callback. \\
Filename bytes & Borrow ends at callback return; copy for retention. \\
Host error & Stored in a rooted or separately owned error slot. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Do Not Treat Unregistration as a Free}

A catalog may have scheduled a callback just before the host closes
its subscription. The subscription needs a closing state and a
native operation that establishes quiescence: the supplier will
not begin another callback and all outstanding invocations are
finished. A counter in the context measures invocations that
already found the context; it cannot account for native code
holding its address but not yet inside the entry. The release
decision therefore cannot rest on observing a zero counter alone.
\Cref{fig:visual-390} separates closing admission from destroying
the native context.

