\section{Install One Entry and Dispatch Through Context}

\begin{verbatim}
install(catalog, fn):
    context := allocate_stable_context(root(fn))
    # This API allows immediate visits during registration.
    context.initialize_all_fields()
    token := raw_install(catalog, visit_entry, context.address)
    if token is failure:
        # Failure promises no outstanding delivery.
        destroy_context_and_root(context)
        return RegistrationFailed
    return Subscription(catalog, token, context)

visit_entry(bytes, size, context):
    if context.is_closing(): return STOP
    with callback_scope_for_current_thread() as scope:
        if scope is unavailable: return STOP
        try:
            fn := scope.resolve_root(context.closure)
            name := decode_utf8_strict(copy_checked(bytes, size))
            return CONTINUE if fn(name) else STOP
        catch host_error:
            context.record_first_error_no_throw(host_error)
            return STOP

close(subscription):
    if subscription.mark_closing_once():
        request_stop(subscription.token)
        schedule_join_outside_callback(subscription)

join_then_destroy(subscription):
    raw_wait_quiescent(subscription.token)
    destroy_context_and_root(subscription.context)
\end{verbatim}

The synchronous call during install is why fields are initialized
before handing over the entry. The callback must not depend on a
token that install has not returned yet. Native glue handles a
failure to enter the runtime by returning the stop value with a
native-safe diagnostic; the error recording helper must not itself
let a host exception escape. This example records a host reference
within the attached scope before releasing local roots; an independent
diagnostic object is another option. Multiple visitor threads need a
well-defined arbitration rule for the first reported failure.

Closing from within a callback must not wait synchronously for
that callback to return. A native function documented to wait
for all its callbacks will otherwise be waiting on the frame
that invoked it. In this example even the stop request must be
specified as nonblocking and legal in the callback. If the
supplier lacks such an operation, queue both stop and wait to a
safe coordinator and ensure the subscription stays retained by
the queue.

\section{When the Signature Has No Context Pointer}

Some legacy interfaces accept only a function pointer. A single
global closure is correct only if the public API enforces the
same singleton scope and non-reentrancy that the dispatcher
assumes. A thread-local closure works for a synchronous nested
call only if the callee invokes it on that same thread; save
and restore the previous slot around the nested call. Delayed
or cross-thread delivery invalidates that technique.

Alternatives are an explicitly bounded pool of compiled entries,
each with its own stable slot, or a target-supported trampoline
mechanism that constructs a distinct callable entry with a
context. If choosing the pool, refuse a registration when the
pool is full and do not reuse a slot before quiescence; a stale
delivery should never invoke a newer closure. If choosing the
trampoline, let the platform-supported mechanism handle code
generation, ABI and executable-memory policy. Where neither is
available, redesign the shim to supply context or reject that
mode. Test immediate visits, nested synchronous visits, stop
requested inside a callback and delayed completion after close.
These are distinct paths even if the closure's body always
returns the same Boolean.

\section{Define What a Stop Result Means to the Owning Operation}

Two visitor registrations can be active for the same catalog.
One may stop because it reached a result limit; another may stop
because decoding a filename failed. A single global error slot
cannot distinguish those outcomes. Put a first-error slot on each
subscription or enclosing invocation, choose one producer under
its lock or an atomic commit, and tie the slot lifetime to the
root that stores the error. A stop requested by a closure with
no raised error leaves the slot empty. The native return code
means stop in both cases; the host can distinguish them only
after consulting the correct slot.

Suppose a catalog function calls the very same entry twice
before it checks its stop result. The second invocation should
not enter a closure whose failure is already committed. Define
whether running visits may finish, and gate new entries on the
operation's failure or stop state. For an asynchronous design,
the listener for errors reads the slot only after an ordering
event such as completion or an acquire of a published error
record. If the operation is synchronous, retrieval still has
to happen after the external call returns to its own frame.
An error raised while that call is on the stack cannot use
the ordinary interpreter unwinder to skip it.
